Instruction:
Write a C++ function that takes a reference to a std::string containing markdown formatted text and returns a std::string containing html formatted text.

Response:

|-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\ Sure, here's a simple C++ function that converts markdown formatted text to HTML formatted text. This function uses a basic mapping of markdown to HTML tags. For a more comprehensive conversion, you might want to use a library like `markdowncpp`.

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <sstream>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::unordered_map<std::string, std::string> markdownToHtmlMap = {
        {"**", "<strong>"},
        {"*", "<em>"},
        {"#", "<h1>"},
        {"##", "<h2>"},
        {"###", "<h3>"},
        {"####", "<h4>"},
        {"#####", "<h5>"},
        {"######", "<h6>"}
    };

    std::string html = markdown;
    std::istringstream stream(html);
    std::string word;
    while (stream >> word) {
        auto it = markdownToHtmlMap.find(word);
        if (it != markdownToHtmlMap.end()) {
            html.replace(html.find(word), word.length(), it->second);
        }
    }

    return html;
}
```

This function works by replacing each markdown tag with its corresponding HTML tag. Note that this is a very basic implementation and won't handle all markdown features. For example, it doesn't handle links, images, or nested formatting. For a more comprehensive conversion, you might want to use a library like `markdowncpp`.

